## Outcome
State the restored behavior and how success is recognized.

## Non-goals
List excluded cleanup, redesign, and unrelated symptoms.

## Requirements Traceability
List each explicit source requirement and point to the reproduction, rule, interface, regression test, or non-goal that addresses it. Do not silently narrow the requested correction.

## Failure and Impact
Describe expected versus actual behavior, affected actors, severity, scope, and environment.

## Reproduction
Give deterministic steps, inputs, preconditions, and observed evidence.

## Root Cause
Explain the violated rule or contract and its owning location. Separate root cause, trigger, symptom, and contributing conditions.

## Regression Test
Identify the test, why its level is reliable, the command, and its expected behavior before and after correction. The CLI records actual results.

## Concrete Examples
Give the failing example plus adjacent happy, edge, and failure cases.

## Domain Concepts, Rules, and Invariants
Define relevant language and the rules the correction must restore. If none, say why.

## Boundaries and Dependencies
Name affected responsibilities and dependency direction. For storage, transport, clocks, identity, messaging, and external services, state the inward-owned contract, outward adapter, composition point, transaction/data ownership, and failure behavior. Justify any direct infrastructure dependency.

## Public Interfaces and Errors
Record compatibility, schemas, commands, APIs, events, and error semantics. If unchanged, say why.

## Correction and Extension Pressure
Describe the correction at the rule-owning level. List affected abstractions and contracts; prefer a narrow inward-owned port where the defect exposes an infrastructure or domain boundary. Record related extension pressure without generic layering.

## Test Traceability
Map root cause, neighboring risks, rules, and boundaries to regression, unit, and integration tests. Explain any gap.

## Implementation Plan
Use `### Slice N: <observable outcome>` subsections. Every slice must include `Outcome`, `Entry point`, `Core behavior`, `Boundary integration`, `Tests`, `Acceptance command`, and `Complete when`. `Acceptance command` is a JSON string array that runs the corrected observable path. Keep the regression lock-in observable and make correction slices runnable across every affected layer.

## Implementation Conformance
Complete during build; record deviations only after design review restart.

